# Ghidra project databases are committed, but they are not text.
#
# A .rep is a mix of binary (the HSQLDB pages, *.gbf) and XML (project.prp and
# the idata/*.prp property files). Without -merge, git is happy to text-merge
# two branches' project.prp on a two-branch merge and produce a project that is
# neither branch's -- a conflict-free merge that silently loses whichever side
# lost. -merge makes git refuse instead, which is the correct outcome: a
# project that two branches both rebuilt has to be re-rebuilt, not merged.
#
# This is not optional decoration; it is the difference between a loud conflict
# and a corrupt database.
*.gpr            binary -diff -merge
*.gbf            binary -diff -merge
**/*.rep/**      binary -diff -merge

# The decompiled C is machine output, and it is digested.
#
# -text, on all three trees: every component's committed .c is LF-only today
# (measured: zero CR bytes across all 2,755 of them), and c-digests.csv records
# a SHA-256 of those exact bytes. Git's default core.autocrlf=true on a Windows
# checkout would convert them to CRLF on the way out, and every digest in the
# repository would then fail for a contributor who did nothing wrong. -text
# makes the digest bytes checkout-stable, which is the whole reason a committed
# digest is affordable here at all.
#
# -diff, on the Windows tree ONLY. The argument is the 56 MB
# GamingCenter3_Cross.c: a one-line change to it would otherwise render as a
# whole-file diff in review, the same reason the Ghidra databases above carry
# -diff. That argument does not reach the other two components -- the EC's 2,710
# .c files are 3.3 MB in total, about 1.2 KB each, and the BIOS's 39 are 1 MB --
# and -diff on a 1.2 KB file is not a large diff, it is NO diff: git prints
# "Binary files differ" and a reviewer sees nothing at all. Applying it to all
# three would have made a decompile change undetectable *in review* rather than
# merely unreviewable, which is the opposite of what the digest is for. So the
# EC and BIOS .c files stay ordinary text and the digest row is what a reviewer
# reads there; the Windows ones are -diff because at 56 MB that is the only
# thing git can render at all.
#
# Note this is `-text` and NOT `binary` anywhere: the files are text, and
# marking them binary would disable the CRLF normalisation git applies to text
# on the way IN, which is a second way for the same digests to stop matching.
ec/decompiled/**/*.c       -text
bios/decompiled/**/*.c     -text
windows/decompiled/**/*.c  -text -diff

# The annotation CSVs a tool byte-compares, which is the same hazard as the
# decompiled .c trees above and a different correct answer.
#
# --check in these tools compares the committed file's bytes against a freshly
# generated table and exits 1 on any difference, so the bytes in the worktree
# are the pass condition (call_graph.py's check_table() is the worked example,
# and its self-test proves a CRLF table is rejected). That makes a
# core.autocrlf=true Windows checkout reachable rather than hypothetical, for
# the same reason and with the same answer: -text and never binary, for the
# reason the warning above gives.
#
# So why two forms rather than one? Because the annotation tree is not one
# thing. The CRLF files are not drift -- they are these tools' correct output:
# every one of walk_budget_census.py, trace_xdata_refs.py, walk_flow_follow.py,
# pd_entry_forms.py, pd_image_census.py, pd_direct_offset_sites.py,
# find_indirect_xdata.py, inc_dptr_sites.py, census_xdata_writers.py and
# code_pointer_sites.py passes a bare csv.writer() no lineterminator, so csv's
# default \r\n is what they render, and each of their --check runs is green
# today. For those, -text is the only correct form: it stops autocrlf
# normalising CRLF->LF on the way IN, which would be a whole-file diff and a
# red check, and it leaves the worktree bytes exactly what the tool wrote.
# Marking one of them `text eol=lf` would send it out as LF and break the green
# check it is protecting.
#
# The LF tables are the other case, and the reason they do NOT get -text is the
# editable surface: ghidra-functions.csv is transcribed by hand (CLAUDE.md), and
# its bank0/0EA2 comment carries an embedded blank line. With -text a Windows
# contributor's edit to that field commits CRLF verbatim and a CR lands inside a
# parsed field; `text eol=lf` normalises it back on commit, so the round trip is
# lossless and the file stays ordinary text for diff and merge.
#
# Both forms renormalise nothing on today's tree, which is why this is an
# attribute change alone. Moving the CRLF tools to lineterminator="\n" and
# renormalising their output is a separate commit with its own diff.
#
# Measured per file by ec/tools/test_gitattributes_coverage.py, which holds the
# rule this comment claims rather than a count of the tree: every byte-compared
# table is covered, no line covering one says binary, and no path matched by an
# eol=lf line carries a CR. See
# docs/findings/annotation-csv-checkout-stability.md.
ec/annotations/*.csv                     -text

# The LF tables, named so the blanket above does not swallow them. Each is a
# tool's --check input or output; the two call_graph.py reads are in it because
# a CRLF checkout of ghidra-functions.csv changes a parsed field there even
# though the rendered table happens to come out byte-identical for this tool.
ec/annotations/call-graph-callees.csv    text eol=lf
ec/annotations/dsdt-ecmg-fields.csv     text eol=lf
ec/annotations/task-call-table.csv       text eol=lf
ec/annotations/bucket-c-codemap.csv      text eol=lf
ec/annotations/site-resolution.csv      text eol=lf
ec/annotations/fan-table-defaults.csv    text eol=lf
ec/annotations/xdata-registers.csv       text eol=lf
ec/annotations/xdata-clusters.csv        text eol=lf
ec/annotations/ghidra-functions.csv      text eol=lf
ec/decompiled/index.csv                  text eol=lf

# The same rule outside the annotations tree: every committed CSV another
# component's tool byte-compares in --check. All are LF today, so all take
# text eol=lf, and test_gitattributes_coverage.py fails if one stops being.
# c-digests.csv is deliberately absent: it is covered by the .c entries above,
# and it is the file those entries exist for.
ec/ghidra/xdata-symbols.csv              text eol=lf
ec/ghidra/gap-citation-scan.csv         text eol=lf
bios/ifr/charge-questions.csv            text eol=lf
windows/ghidra/c-census.csv              text eol=lf
